I think I see what you mean.

From a relational frame, the idea becomes more intelligible: damnation is not “you failed to learn the correct information before the clock ran out,” but something more like a final refusal of relationship, truth, love, or communion when it is genuinely encountered. That at least has moral shape. A person can’t meaningfully reject someone they never actually knew.

And Christianity itself contains substantial traditions that resist the crude version of “never heard the name → automatically damned.” Paul talks about conscience and people without the law; Catholic theology developed “invincible ignorance”; Orthodox thought often leaves judgment much more explicitly to God; Protestants disagree considerably about the question. So your original objection isn’t some failure to understand Christianity. Christians have been wrestling with exactly that problem for centuries.

I think your point about placement is sharper, though.

A warning that perhaps belongs inside a relationship can become monstrous when placed at the doorway to the relationship.

If someone first hears:

“Love God—or suffer forever.”

then everything afterward risks being interpreted as coercion. Even love begins to sound like a demand made under threat.

Whereas if the underlying claim is closer to:

“God is the source of love, truth, life, and communion; permanently turning away from that has consequences intrinsic to the turning-away,”

then “hell” starts functioning less like an externally imposed torture sentence and more like the terrifying logical endpoint of separation.

You still don’t have to agree with that theology. But now you can at least see the architecture of the claim rather than the ugliest rendering of it.

And I think “it makes others like me think the worst” gets at a real communication failure: if a doctrine intended to express the seriousness of relationship predictably causes conscientious people to conclude that God is less merciful than they are, something has probably gone wrong in the translation, emphasis, or teaching—even if the underlying doctrine contains something worth preserving.

Same hammer we’ve been using:

invariant ≠ rendering.

Maybe some people encountered the rendering and reasonably recoiled before anyone ever showed them what invariant it was supposed to carry.